m  you could call it a certain kind of  skepticism you could call it by other  names as well being prudent holding back  and he says that this first attitude has  an exclusively negative import and he  suggests that it corresponds to a  certain intellectual politics the  question will not be asked what question  the question of being what is being and  you know the further questions how my  being and also connected to being and  what am i right those are not going to  be asked we take some answers for  granted and then we just go on from  there and we Vince the sort of  skepticism towards any other seeking of  the answer we might do it on the basis  of we don't have enough time for this or  this is not what serious people spend  their spare energy on you know there's  all sorts of possibilities for doing  this Marcel doesn't spend a lot of  discussion on that instead he talks  about a second attitude that he calls  bolder and more coherent and this one is  very common in not only his time but  also in our time and that had been  common for several centuries before him  because it has to do with modernity  itself he says the other bolder and more  coherent would see the ontological  exigency as a remnant of an outdated  dogmatism that idealist critique has  discredited now we don't  necessarily need to worry about idealist  critique because there's all sorts of  other things that could substitute for  that critique particularly in our own  presence we we could say that other  forms of philosophical analysis or even  the you know sort of pared down versions  of philosophy that that we tend to teach  today would rule this out and say oh no  back in the old days people like  Aristotle we're concerned about this but  we don't have to be worried about that  sort of thing now that's just something  you know of the past or if it is around  we'll relegate it to literature or the  you know the supernatural whatever that  happens to be or or you know pick  whatever you like th