ying that  there's anything like being in the sense  that he means it he also says that they  have a number of different features they  tend to deny the transcendent and try to  reduce it to caricatures that  misrepresent it's essential  characteristics anything that would be  beyond it it says put in my terms or  shut the hell up because you know unless  you're expressing it in terms of you  know back in the old days German  idealism in its language or analytic  philosophy when it's focusing on  language unless you can express it in  terms of language games or pick whatever  you want now  you know possible world semantics or you  know the latest Continental critique  jargony rich in many ways pick pick  anything you like it's the same attitude  fit it into my boxes or it doesn't exist  and that denies any possibility for the  transcendent he also says that it  ignores the personal in all of its forms  it it excludes the personal the personal  can't really matter even those who today  would say oh the personal is is public  or the personal is political they don't  mean the personal in the same respect  that we do they often mean the personal  blown up into something like you know a  public facing image which is not the  same thing as the individual human being  he says it also ignores the tragic and  this is something quite important and  you might expand this further and say it  even ignores the dramatic in all of its  sense and that's important for Marcel a  philosophy that cannot take account of  our dramatic situation as human actors  is deficient in many respects that is  one main way that we experience what  he's calling this need for being I do  also want to mention his discussion here  of the Cartesian coach Ito he he brings  this up because he says when we start  reflecting on this on the ontological  need there's a paradox here to formulate  this engages me in a labyrinth of  problems does being exist what is being  but I can't reflect on these without a  new abyss opening up beneath my feet and  what is that abyss my questioning myself  what am i what kind of being am i what  what being do I have and he says surely  I who articulate this problem I should  be able to remain outside of it whether  this be before beyond the problem and he  says actually no and this is why the  Cartesian coach Ito as brilliant as it  is does not provide us with the  grounding that we need in order to get  get beyond this because it's not a  matter of getting beyond it's not a  matter of solving a problem so he says  despite what Descartes might have  thought if he gives us an indubitable  the sole certainty which it provides  concerns only the epistemological  subject as the organ of objective  cognition Marcel says it does guard the  threshold of validity there is something  important about the I am or the I exist  but there's more to me and there's more  to me in relation to being as a being  among beings and a being in relation to  being so he says one of the errors  that's involved here is  taking the knowing subject as the human  being arbitrarily separating the  question am i from the ontological  problem taken as a holy sis neither of  these can be approached in isolation  they can only be approached jointly and  simultaneously and that goes against the  Cartesian method of breaking everything  down into its subproblems and solving  each of them in turn which is great for  some things but doesn't work for what  Marcel is talking about here he also  says that the Cartesian position is  inseparable from a dualism that he  rejects without hesitation to pose the  ontological problem is to inquire about  the totality of being and about myself  as a totality so he's rejecting any sort  of dualism whether it be a body and mind  whether it be of self and world or self  and other these are not going to work  for him and so he suggests also that  that vitalism of different sorts is not  going to be the way through here as well  and to bring this sort of two to a close  the fundamental problem as Marcel sees  it is treating being itself as if it's a  problem and now I'm using the word  problem here in in a sort of ironic way  for Marcel there's going to be a vital  distinction between a problem which is  something that can be solved with some  sort of technique some sort of  discipline and can be considered apart  from the subjects involvement in it and  a mystery which is in some respects a  meta problem a problem at a  problematized problem if you like but  one that actually brings in the person  who is examining it within the scope of  the inquiry and the ontological need or  demand or exigency is only going to be  able to be understood from a perspective  that views  as a mystery rather than a problem and  all of these mistaken approaches that  we've looked at treat it as a problem  you
 

